Add the following code to your existing `server` block:
```nginx
# Add post links
location ~ ^/post/(\d+) {
    try_files /post/$1.html @404;
}

location @404 {
    return 404;
}

location ^~ /post {
    index index.html;
    try_files $uri $uri/ /post/index.html;
}
```
Here's what each part of the code does:

1. `location ~ ^/post/(\d+) { ... }` matches any URL that starts with `/post/` followed by a group of one or more digits (`(\d+)`). This will match the URL of each post.
2. `try_files /post/$1.html @404;` attempts to serve a file with the same name as the matched group (`$1`) in the `/post` directory. If the file is not found, it will redirect to the @404 location (which returns a 404 error page).
3. `location @404 { return 404; }` is a catch-all location that returns a 404 error page if the requested URL doesn't match any of the previous conditions.
4. `location ^~ /post { ... }` matches any URL that starts with `/post`. This is used to serve the index file (`index.html`) for the `/post` directory.
5. `index index.html;` sets the default file to serve when someone visits the `/post` directory.
6. `try_files $uri $uri/ /post/index.html;` attempts to serve a file with the same name as the requested URL (`$uri`) in the `/post` directory. If the file is not found, it will serve the `index.html` file instead.

After adding this code, Nginx will use the `/post` directory to serve individual post pages. For example, if you have a post with the URL `/post/123`, Nginx will serve the file `/usr/share/nginx/html/post/123.html`. If the file doesn't exist, it will return a 404 error.

Note that you'll need to create HTML files for each post in the `/post` directory for this to work. You can generate these files automatically using a script or create them manually.

